1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,599
A trucker app asks for parcel PRC-90210.
The id does not exist. What should the
JSON look like — and will every other
missing-id path in the harbor API look the
same?

2
00:00:12,779 --> 00:00:24,179
Without a shared approach, each controller
invents an answer. One returns a string.
Another returns a Map. A third lets Tomcat
render an HTML error page to a JSON
client.

3
00:00:24,299 --> 00:00:27,219
Operators cannot alert on status codes
consistently.

4
00:00:27,339 --> 00:00:35,059
Spring MVC gives you structured hooks:
@ExceptionHandler on a controller,
@RestControllerAdvice for application-wide
handlers,

5
00:00:35,179 --> 00:00:41,899
and ResponseEntityExceptionHandler as a
base when you want to customize framework
exceptions such as bind failures.

6
00:00:42,019 --> 00:00:58,299
Start from a domain signal and a stable
error shape — Problem+JSON style fields
work well even if you keep a simple
record: Look at this java for a moment.
Look at this java for a moment. Look at
this java for a moment.

7
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:07,379
Controllers stay thin: throw
ParcelNotFoundException when a lookup
misses; do not build error maps inline.
Advice owns status and envelope.

8
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:21,099
Validation failures from the previous
lesson become first-class citizens of the
same bridge — clients parse one shape for
"bad customs JSON" and "unknown parcel
id." @ResponseStatus on an exception type
can set the code,

9
00:01:21,219 --> 00:01:25,219
but a status without a body is a weak
contract for mobile clients.

10
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:28,579
Prefer advice that always returns the
shared problem type.

11
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:41,140
Local @ExceptionHandler methods on a
single controller are fine for controller-
specific cases; application-wide policy
belongs on @RestControllerAdvice so
luggage, parcels, and customs do not
diverge.

12
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:54,060
Wrapping every controller method in
try/catch "for safety" scatters policy and
guarantees inconsistency. Returning
different JSON shapes per exception type
with no shared fields forces every client
to write N parsers.

13
00:01:54,180 --> 00:02:04,580
Swallowing exceptions and returning 200
with "success": false teaches clients to
ignore HTTP — reverse that habit early.
Failures now have a deliberate model.

14
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:16,819
Some concerns still must wrap the request
*before* Spring MVC even picks a handler —
encoding, security filters, correlation
ids at the servlet boundary. That layer is
the servlet Filter chain.
